  "worked_solution": "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}).\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(y\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x+x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y \\\\ \\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y} & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(y\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x+x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y+1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x+\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the partial derivatives at $(x,y)=(1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x}(1,1) & =\\frac{1}{1}+\\operatorname{ln}\u20611=1 \\\\ \\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}(1,1) & =\\operatorname{ln}\u20611+\\frac{1}{1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the equation of the tangent plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-1 & =(x-1)\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x}(1,1)+(y-1)\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y}(1,1) \\\\ z-1 & =1\\cdot (x-1)+1\\cdot (y-1) \\\\ z-1 & =x+y-2 \\\\ -x-y+z+1 & =0 \\\\ x+y-z-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
